#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/308390111ffcb243fb8612b873317302c966df288001b28c6d9b02f5946aac94.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/a7df5d5b19c15a0e5f7b9d1dc93d810ca41b70c84d28c770a0d15521b14d1374.png) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/e13b964d4391f86125a17302c70af51040c331eb76393ebb39a1fef65dab569d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/ea1fe153d54f6a769e3d13177bfede1eb467525feb255fbcfd7ec88efe58b733.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/075599a7512d1105b9c6ad3acf3f4d043cb60c2aedeb8f53532045390c29f1e9.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/73b2b16a58acd290d040047339b0db3c79de19c0af04303e010a2d16e75bb94b.png) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/c5383ad51988564756ba754cbd6107f487fd0b32be0013f8a89bb78a6900f540.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257" :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/1d282bb39042b44ed346503f3c7c4213b02089bbc7d833e82a5d95eb49f4db28.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/a23531cb53a9b53f47d8a7080af7de78e6d7cdd7fa3a3632c37a720c7e8ba92c.png)\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/ca123e2e8451fab6c7b106a5d1a05d153020854c694ec41cfb5d38de2fb3ae5f.png)\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204a5268-f663-570c-9034-4c2462f3cf16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348916} {:answer/id #uuid "5b64dac8-3629-509d-bd01-6eddffc90d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348917} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf63f2b-adcb-5790-a908-197755efe8e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348918} {:answer/id #uuid "fa47b83b-00b1-5ee4-b1eb-2a596070170b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348919} {:answer/id #uuid "896928e3-84d1-5e3a-8a5b-a7bd0278686e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf63f2b-adcb-5790-a908-197755efe8e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348918} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd9b28be-6188-52d1-b451-8783cf54237b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b32c3e49-95fd-5233-9aa7-ff2e20c39b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110754" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348921 :knowledge-point/_questions 