0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6de3d68f-8a22-57ec-a97f-0d2859f05540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348930} {:answer/id #uuid "c5826d1f-1a21-516b-8ddd-b256bde77ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348931} {:answer/id #uuid "7b36d5e2-41b0-5a86-a0b4-bba901994148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348932} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bbd7de-ad84-5762-b488-137055ba6643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348933} {:answer/id #uuid "a052dc38-55e9-5c38-8cdd-5e466e1db11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b36d5e2-41b0-5a86-a0b4-bba901994148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348932} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ababff-6b6a-5ca8-932f-a1b5b28b901f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcfe1125-e3c9-5d59-baa8-6c9eb6d6e0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-110850" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=0\\cdot {a}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1237d624-03f6-5a4e-8246-973ef4b5246c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348937} {:answer/id #uuid "cea3a04d-8bf2-59d3-830e-cfe3f74d3c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} {:answer/id #uuid "1c228d93-2d2d-5bf5-81bc-2e83fde2212d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348939} {:answer/id #uuid "1457ea26-7280-5675-88c8-99a63f49b6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348940} {:answer/id #uuid "3e08923d-bcef-58d2-aa69-b274835ac8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1237d624-03f6-5a4e-8246-973ef4b5246c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348937} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9b2918-bf91-5123-8e44-9946d212659f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a050121e-906c-5e75-b97c-bebdab51add7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110853" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe3a446-5618-5621-b4f5-39feb5214d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348944} {:answer/id #uuid "a67fcef5-6a6b-5dea-a779-51db1fe83c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348945} {:answer/id #uuid "9900986f-d09f-5168-ad40-61f805f28e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348946} {:answer/id #uuid "478a4df8-9d20-5824-a82c-9be96d9d7db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348947} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec65416-0d63-5292-92bd-999a3814f60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "478a4df8-9d20-5824-a82c-9be96d9d7db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348947} :answer-field/id #uuid "07074f8b-e3b8-5c50-8d2f-69ee4582070d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ee4526d-564d-5328-b11a-a4978f3ab8b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110909" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second row of the RREF matrix above, we see that the system of equations has no solution.\n\nTherefore, ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7b242f0-d224-5eba-88ba-c22ab0a2851b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348951} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e896ce-0227-5f49-b81d-e4de50e47342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348952} {:answer/id #uuid "769d7df4-e981-5de5-a227-c808dc86c355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348953} {:answer/id #uuid "b10f180c-aef8-52cd-a48f-673e7ccc49c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348954} {:answer/id #uuid "9393a102-025b-55b1-842c-d8eefcd4dbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9393a102-025b-55b1-842c-d8eefcd4dbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348955} :answer-field/id #uuid "3487d8b6-7685-5a2b-9d22-6837b3e87883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61b7884b-8bb2-5c64-8e97-dc45271c3539" :question/math-academy-id "q-110972" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-29 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 6 & -29 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=-2,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-3)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-1)+(-2)+(-3)=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd5fe755-84a3-511b-a9b0-ba292895d1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348958} {:answer/id #uuid "a1927ec8-8552-5bc2-b7bb-570dc9f13259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348959} {:answer/id #uuid "ac57c1f9-fafb-55ee-a274-595deddb1f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{ali